course: biology
lesson: 385
title: Ice Age Ukraine and Europe: cave bears, cave lions, the Siberian unicorn and mammoth-bone houses
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: About 15,000 years ago, at Mezhyrich in Ukraine, people built round huts from mammoth jaws, skulls, tusks and long bones. There were few trees on the cold steppe, so giant bones were the best building material they had.
explain: Much of Ukraine was then a cold, dry grassland called the mammoth steppe. Long winters and dry winds kept trees away, but grass and herbs fed huge herds of mammoths, horses, bison and woolly rhinos.
explain: Hunters lived there too: cave lions, cave hyenas that dragged bones to their dens, and people. Cave bears slept through the winter in caves, and many never woke up, so some caves hold the bones of thousands.
explain: When the Ice Age ended, the climate warmed fast, grassland turned to forest and people hunted more. The animals built for the cold steppe disappeared, though scientists still argue how much was climate and how much was people.
explain: Bones, frozen bodies, cave paintings and chewed scraps are the clues that let us picture this lost world.
fact: Cave bears lived in Ice Age Europe, were bigger than most brown bears, ate mostly plants and died out about 25,000 years ago; many died in their winter sleep in caves.
fact: Cave lions lived in cold Europe and Asia; cave paintings show them without big manes, though we cannot be sure the males had none.
fact: Frozen cave lion cubs were found in the ice of Siberia with their fur still on.
fact: Elasmotherium, the Siberian unicorn, was a giant rhino of the grasslands of Ukraine, Russia and Central Asia; only a bump on its skull shows where a big horn sat, and it was reported to live until about 39,000 years ago.
fact: Rhino horns are made of keratin, the same stuff as hair and nails, so they rot away and rarely become fossils.
fact: The giant deer, often called the Irish elk, was not an elk and lived across Europe and Asia, but many of its best skeletons were dug out of Irish bogs; its antlers could be about three and a half metres wide, and males grew a new pair every year.
fact: At Mezhyrich in Ukraine, about 15,000 years ago, people built huts from mammoth bones, with jaws stacked in a neat zigzag.
fact: A mammoth chewed with four big grinding teeth at a time, one in each half of each jaw, and had two tusks; worn teeth were replaced about six times in a life.
fact: Cave hyenas cracked bones with powerful jaws and dragged them to their dens, which scientists find by gnawed bones and fossil droppings.
fact: Ice Age Ukraine was mostly cold dry grassland, the mammoth steppe, and the steppe bison was bigger than today's bison with longer horns.
fact: At the end of the Ice Age the climate warmed fast, grasslands became forest and people hunted more; how much each mattered is still argued.
fact: The last mammoths lived on a small Arctic island until about 4,000 years ago, when the pyramids of Egypt were already built.
exercise: What did cave bears mostly eat, scientists think? | Mostly plants | Mostly fish | Only mammoths | Only honey
exercise: Why do some caves hold the bones of thousands of cave bears? | Bears slept in them over thousands of years and some died there | A flood washed them all into the cave at once on one terrible day | People collected them for a museum | Hyenas carried every bear there alive
exercise: What do cave paintings show about cave lions? | They had no big manes | They had stripes like tigers | They had long trunks | They lived in trees
exercise: What were found in the ice of Siberia? | Frozen cave lion cubs with fur still on | Living cave lions | Cave lion eggs frozen in a nest | Paintings of cartoon lions on the walls
exercise: What was the Siberian unicorn really? | A giant rhino called Elasmotherium | A white horse with a long spiral horn | A kind of narwhal | A giant deer
exercise: Why has the Siberian unicorn's horn never been found? | Rhino horn is like hair and nails and rots away | It was made of gold and stolen by robbers long ago | It was hidden in the sea | It never had any horn
exercise: Where did the Siberian unicorn live? | On grasslands of Ukraine, Russia and Central Asia | In the jungles of Brazil | In Antarctica, under the ice | Only in Ireland and the islands nearby
exercise: How wide could the giant deer's antlers be? | About three and a half metres | About thirty centimetres | About ten metres | About one metre
exercise: Why is the giant deer called the Irish elk? | Many good skeletons came from Irish bogs | It lived only in Ireland and nowhere else | It was a real elk | It was tamed by Irish farmers to pull their carts
exercise: How often did a male giant deer grow new antlers? | Every year | Once in its life | Every ten years | Never, they kept growing
exercise: What were the huts at Mezhyrich built from? | Mammoth bones | Bricks and glass | Oak logs | Stone blocks
exercise: Why did people at Mezhyrich build with bones instead of wood? | There were few trees on the cold steppe | Bones are lighter than wood | Wood was against their rules and their gods | They liked white houses
exercise: How many grinding teeth did a mammoth use at once? | Four | Two | Thirty-two | Twelve
exercise: How do scientists find an old hyena den? | By gnawed bones and fossil hyena droppings | By hyena paintings on the walls | By hyena footprints in rock only | By finding hyena nests of sticks
exercise: What was the mammoth steppe? | A cold dry grassland across much of Ukraine | A thick jungle full of mammoths and monkeys | A frozen sea | A desert of sand dunes
exercise: Why could the steppe feed huge herds? | Grass and herbs grew everywhere and fed grazers | It rained sweets | Trees dropped fruit all year long, even in the frozen winter | The animals ate snow
test: Count them: a mammoth has 2 tusks and 4 grinding teeth at once. How many tusks and grinding teeth do 3 mammoths have together? | 6 tusks and 12 grinding teeth | 2 tusks and 4 grinding teeth | 6 tusks and 6 grinding teeth | 3 tusks and 12 grinding teeth
test: Count them: a giant deer has 2 antlers, 4 legs and 1 tail. How many antlers, legs and tails on 2 deer? | 4 antlers, 8 legs, 2 tails | 2 antlers, 4 legs, 1 tail | 4 antlers, 4 legs, 4 tails | 8 antlers, 8 legs, 8 tails
test: In the Ice Age films Manny the mammoth lives next to Diego the sabre-tooth and Sid the sloth. A friend says mammoths had loads of small teeth like us. What is right? | A mammoth chewed with four huge grinding teeth at a time | Mammoths had thirty-two small teeth | Mammoths had no teeth, only tusks | Mammoths had sharp fangs like lions
test: «unicorns are fake, so the Siberian unicorn is fake too» What is right? | It was a real giant rhino, only the name is a joke | Yes, it is only a fairy tale | It was a real white horse with a horn on its head | It is still alive in Siberia
test: «dude all lions got manes» What do Ice Age paintings suggest? | Cave lions may have had no big manes | Cave lions had the biggest manes ever | Lions never lived in Europe | Only female lions had manes
test: What would happen to mammoth steppe animals if their grassland turned into thick forest? | They would lose their food and space and struggle | They would thrive and grow bigger | Nothing, they ate trees anyway | They would turn into forest animals overnight
test: «a hut made of bones?? sounds like a horror film» Why did it make sense? | Trees were scarce on the steppe and bones were strong | People wanted to scare visitors | Bones were softer than wood | Bones were easy to buy in shops in the nearest big town
test: Grandpa: «mammoths all died before the pyramids, long before». What is right? | The last mammoths lived until about 4,000 years ago, after the pyramids | Yes, millions of years before | Mammoths were alive when cars were invented and people took photos of them | Mammoths never died out
test: «found a cave full of chewed old bones and old poo, whose?» What is the best guess for an Ice Age cave? | A hyena den | A cave bear's kitchen | A mammoth nursery | A lion's painting studio
test: «just one reason the mammoths died, climate, done» What is the fair view? | Climate and people both mattered and scientists argue how much | It was only a giant meteor | It was only one big flood | People had nothing to do with it ever, because they could not hunt such huge animals
test: Why is the giant deer not really an elk? | It was a separate kind of deer, named from Irish bog finds | It was a horse with antlers | It was a cow | It was a moose that lived in Ireland only and nowhere else in Europe
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which mammoth bones were employed in the construction of dwellings at Mezhyrich. | Timber was scarce upon the treeless steppe | Bone was regarded as sacred and wood forbidden | Bone weighs less than any timber | Stone was abundant but too soft
exam: State the number of grinding teeth employed simultaneously by a single mammoth. | Four | Two | Six | Thirty-two
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Elasmotherium, hereinafter termed the Siberian unicorn. | It was a giant rhinoceros whose horn has not been preserved | It was a horse bearing a single horn | It survives at present in Siberia | It was a marine mammal of the Arctic
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the causes of the disappearance of the mammoth-steppe fauna? | Rapid warming, the spread of forest and human hunting, in disputed proportions | Exclusively the impact of a single meteorite | Exclusively a sudden worldwide flood | Exclusively a disease brought by migrating birds from the southern continents
